A||||3||DenisBisantz||'87 vs.'88 GT Mechanical Differences||||1||
Z||000000||DenisBisantz||11-18-2001||02:46 PM||||What are the significant mechanical differences between the 1987 and 1988 Fiero GT models?  Aside from the one year difference in car age, is one model year more preferred over the other? ||||reg||1
Z||000001||Cooter||11-18-2001||03:04 PM||||Welcome to the Forum!<BR>Now, body wise, they are pretty much the same. But the 88 has completly different suspension and brakes. In stock form the 88's handle better, have improved brakes and might have a slightly softer ride, but more aftermarket items are available for the 84-87's which make it better than the stock 88. Also, because of the one year only production run on the 88 components, they are typically more expensive and harder to find.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Monkeyman||11-18-2001||06:59 PM||||I've never owned an '88, but I'm pretty sure they're internally balanced (something like that).||||reg||1
Z||000003||tgowens||11-18-2001||09:33 PM||||The '88 engine is internally balanced with a neutrally balanced flywheel. The '87s back use a balanced flywheel. The suspension setup is different for the '88 inasmuch as it ises a tri-arm/trailing arm suspension versus the '87s back a-arm suspension. Larger vented brake rotors are used on the '88 as well as a redesigned brake caliper and hub units - these are not interchangeable with the '87s back. The '88 uses a different oil sending unit which is smaller than the '87s back -- those are just the ones off of the top of my head -- there are other minot differences among them.<P>Personally I prefer the '87s because of the parts availability - I may sacrifice a little in handling but with the fact that many of the parts for the '88 were only made for one year they are becoming scarce.<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/signature_TGO.jpg"> <P><A HREF="http://www.cfog-i.org" TARGET=_blank>Classic Fiero Owners Group International</A>||||reg||1
